Each case ENCODES a PNG by driving the WRITE side of libpng and then records the produced bytes. The general shape is:

```rust
fn low_all_shapes(api: &Api, rng: &mut Rng) {
    unsafe {
        for (color, depth) in A1 {                 // 15 legal color/depth combos
            for (w, h) in SHAPES {                 // 10 shapes incl. sub-byte row ends
                let mut wr = Writer::new(api);     // fresh write struct, output -> wbuf()
                (api.png_set_IHDR)(wr.png, wr.info, w, h, depth as c_int, color as c_int,
                                   PNG_INTERLACE_NONE, PNG_COMPRESSION_TYPE_BASE,
                                   PNG_FILTER_TYPE_BASE);
                if color == 3 { /* png_set_PLTE with 1<<depth entries from rng */ }
                (api.png_write_info)(wr.png, wr.info);
                let rb = rowbytes_of(w, depth, color);
                for _ in 0..h {
                    let row = rng.image_bytes(rb);
                    (api.png_write_row)(wr.png, row.as_ptr());
                }
                (api.png_write_end)(wr.png, wr.info);
                emit_bytes(&format!("png ct={} bd={} {}x{}", color, depth, w, h), wbuf());
                wr.destroy();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

STEP 3 - HARD RULES (violating any of these makes the file useless):
1. NEVER call anything from the `png` crate directly. The ONLY way to reach libpng is `(api.png_xxx)(...)`. There is no other legal call.
2. ALL observations go through `p!("...", ..)`, `emit("..")`, or `emit_bytes(tag, bytes)` from support.rs. Do not use println!, dbg!, or eprintln!.
3. The output transcript MUST be deterministic and identical between two runs of the same library. Therefore NEVER print a raw pointer value, an address, a memory size that could differ, or anything time-derived. Print `.is_null()` instead of a pointer. For byte buffers use `emit_bytes`.
4. A `png_error` inside libpng TERMINATES the worker process (the harness error callback records the message then exits). So a case must not expect anything after a fatal error. These write cases are VALID-INPUT cases, so they should not trigger errors at all - if one does, that itself is a real finding, so just let it happen.
5. `Writer::new(api)` resets the output buffer, so call `emit_bytes(..., wbuf())` before creating the next Writer, and always `wr.destroy()` when done.
6. Signature of every case fn: `fn name(api: &Api, rng: &mut Rng)`. Get the rng in `run` with `let mut rng = Rng::new(seed);`.
7. Use `support::variant()` / `pick(&[...])` only if a case genuinely needs one-fatal-per-process. These write cases should not need it.
8. Keep every case FAST: total under ~2 seconds. Use image sizes <= 64x64. Do not use 1000000-pixel images.
9. `png_set_*` string arguments need a NUL-terminated buffer: use `let k = cs("Title"); ... k.as_ptr() as *mut c_char`. Keep the `cs()` Vec alive for the whole call.
10. Every case must actually produce output lines; a case that emits nothing is worthless.

