The general shape of a read case: build a valid PNG byte stream, hand it to a Reader, apply the transform under test, decode every row, and record the decoded bytes plus the reported metadata:

```rust
fn low_all(api: &Api, rng: &mut Rng) {
    unsafe {
        for (color, depth) in A1 {
            for (w, h) in SHAPES {
                let data = build_png(rng, w, h, depth, color, &[], &[]);
                let mut rd = Reader::new(api, data);
                (api.png_read_info)(rd.png, rd.info);
                p!("ct={} bd={} {}x{} rowbytes={} channels={}",
                   (api.png_get_color_type)(rd.png, rd.info),
                   (api.png_get_bit_depth)(rd.png, rd.info),
                   (api.png_get_image_width)(rd.png, rd.info),
                   (api.png_get_image_height)(rd.png, rd.info),
                   (api.png_get_rowbytes)(rd.png, rd.info),
                   (api.png_get_channels)(rd.png, rd.info));
                (api.png_read_update_info)(rd.png, rd.info);
                let rb = (api.png_get_rowbytes)(rd.png, rd.info);
                let mut all = Vec::new();
                for _ in 0..h {
                    let mut row = vec![0u8; rb + 8];
                    (api.png_read_row)(rd.png, row.as_mut_ptr(), null());
                    all.extend_from_slice(&row[..rb]);
                }
                (api.png_read_end)(rd.png, rd.info);
                emit_bytes(&format!("rows ct={} bd={} {}x{}", color, depth, w, h), &all);
                rd.destroy();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

NOTE: `build_png(rng, w, h, depth, color, extra_before_idat, extra_after_idat)` already builds a complete valid non-interlaced PNG (with a PLTE when color==3) using stored-deflate IDAT. For interlaced input you must build the stream yourself with `chunk()`, `ihdr_data()`, `zlib_store()` and the Adam7 pass geometry, OR - simpler and preferred - ENCODE it first with the write API into `wbuf()` and then feed those bytes to the reader. Encoding first is the recommended approach for every case that needs a non-trivial input, because it also exercises the writer.

