These are ERROR-PATH cases: each one hand-builds a MALFORMED PNG byte stream, feeds it to the read API, and records what libpng reports. The harness error callback prints `ERROR <message>` and exits with status 90; the warning callback prints `WARN  <message>` and continues. The driver compares the whole transcript AND the exit status between C and Rust, so "both rejected it the same way with the same message" is the literal assertion.

CRITICAL STRUCTURAL RULE - one fatal error per process:
A `png_error` never returns; the worker process ends. So a case that must probe N independent fatal conditions MUST use variants. `support::variant()` returns the variant index for this process and `pick(&[a,b,c])` selects `variant() % 3`. Write the case as:

```rust
fn ihdr_fields(api: &Api, rng: &mut Rng) {
    // one malformed IHDR per process
    let bad: &[(&str, u32, u32, u8, u8, u8, u8, u8)] = &[
        ("w=0",        0, 4, 8, 2, 0, 0, 0),
        ("w>2^31",     0x80000000, 4, 8, 2, 0, 0, 0),
        ("w>userlimit",1000001, 4, 8, 2, 0, 0, 0),
        ("h=0",        4, 0, 8, 2, 0, 0, 0),
        /* ... one row per ERRORS.md row C60..C72 ... */
    ];
    let (name, w, h, d, c, comp, filt, il) = pick(bad);
    p!("case {}", name);
    let mut s = Vec::new();
    s.extend_from_slice(&PNG_SIG);
    s.extend_from_slice(&chunk(b"IHDR", &ihdr_data(w, h, d, c, comp, filt, il)));
    s.extend_from_slice(&chunk(b"IEND", &[]));
    unsafe {
        let mut rd = Reader::new(api, s);
        (api.png_read_info)(rd.png, rd.info);
        emit("no error");        // reaching here at all is itself compared
        rd.destroy();
    }
}
```
