- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-uuPAHO/c_src/include/pngpriv.h and the matching c_src/src/*.c   <- for the CONTRACT of each internal function (what it does, what state it needs)

STEP 2 - WHAT THE FILE MUST CONTAIN

A single `pub fn run(api: &Api, case: &str, seed: u64) -> bool` dispatching to one fn per case (exactly like prim.rs), returning false for unknown names. It must handle these 27 case names:

ll/math_fixed ll/gamma_correct ll/srgb_tables ll/colorspace_xy ll/fp_strings ll/ascii_from ll/safecat_format ll/check_keyword ll/row_bgr_invert_swap ll/row_strip_channel ll/row_read_interlace ll/row_write_interlace ll/read_filter_row ll/check_ihdr_direct ll/chunk_unknown_handling ll/user_version_check ll/zalloc ll/malloc_internals ll/set_text_2 ll/warning_params ll/app_error ll/filter_heuristics ll/create_png_struct ll/io_direct ll/crc_direct ll/write_chunks_direct ll/error_dispatch

These cases call libpng's LOWEST-LEVEL exported entry points DIRECTLY (they are exported because this build has no visibility script, so they are part of the ABI under test). Purpose: pinpoint a divergence to a single leaf function instead of only seeing it through the whole pipeline.

PER-CASE CONTENT:
- ll/math_fixed: png_muldiv (out-param + return), png_reciprocal, png_reciprocal2, png_fixed, png_fixed_ITU. Use boundary values (0, 1, -1, i32::MAX, i32::MIN, i32::MIN+1, 100000, 50000, 21474, 21475, -21474, -21475, 214748, 214749) plus >=2000 randomized values. png_fixed / png_fixed_ITU take a png_ptr and png_error on overflow, so ONLY pass in-range doubles here (out-of-range is covered elsewhere).
- ll/gamma_correct: png_gamma_significant over boundary + random fixed-point gammas; png_gamma_8bit_correct for ALL 256 values x a set of gammas; png_gamma_16bit_correct for a spread of values x gammas; png_gamma_correct on a read struct after png_read_info.
- ll/srgb_tables: use api.data_tables(). Read png_sRGB_table[0..256] (u16), png_sRGB_base[0..512] (u16), png_sRGB_delta[0..512] (u8) and emit_bytes each one's raw little-endian byte image. Element-by-element equality is the assertion.
- ll/colorspace_xy: png_XYZ_from_xy and png_xy_from_XYZ. Feed the sRGB primaries, degenerate all-zero input, and >=500 randomized png_xy / png_XYZ values; print the int return AND every field of the out-struct.
- ll/fp_strings: png_check_fp_number (print return, *statep and *whereami) and png_check_fp_string over a hand-list of valid/invalid numeric strings ("1", "1.0", "-1.5e10", "+.5", ".", "e5", "1e", "1e+", "", "0", "00", "1.2.3", " 1", "1 ", "1e999999", "inf", "nan") plus randomized short ASCII strings.
- ll/ascii_from: png_ascii_from_fp and png_ascii_from_fixed writing into a buffer; print the resulting C string and the whole buffer. Use precision 1..=15 and a spread of values. NOTE both png_error on "ASCII conversion buffer too small", so always pass a big enough buffer (>=64 bytes).
- ll/safecat_format: png_safecat with varying bufsize/pos/strings (including truncation), printing the returned position and buffer contents; png_format_number with every `format` value libpng defines (see PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_* in pngpriv.h) over boundary + random numbers.
- ll/check_keyword: png_check_keyword on a write struct, with keywords that are empty, 1 char, 79 chars, 80 chars, leading/trailing spaces, internal double spaces, control chars (0x01, 0x1f, 0x7f), high-bit chars, and randomized ones. Print the returned length and the resulting new_key buffer. It emits warnings, which are part of the transcript.
- ll/row_bgr_invert_swap: png_do_bgr, png_do_invert, png_do_swap, png_do_packswap. Build a png_row_info by hand for each of the 15 A1 color/depth combos (set width, rowbytes = rowbytes_of(w,depth,color), color_type, bit_depth, channels = channels_of(color), pixel_depth = channels*depth), fill the row from rng, call the function, emit_bytes the mutated row. Use several widths.
- ll/row_strip_channel: png_do_strip_channel with at_start = 0 and 1, over the color types that have >=2 channels.
- ll/row_read_interlace: png_do_read_interlace(row_info, row, pass, transformations) for pass 0..6, every A1 combo, transformations 0 and PNG_TRANSFORM-ish bit patterns. IMPORTANT: this function EXPANDS the row in place, so allocate the row buffer at full output width (rowbytes for the full image width plus slack) and set row_info.width to the sub-image width for that pass, exactly as libpng's own caller does - read c_src/src/pngrutil.c png_do_read_interlace and its caller to get this right, otherwise BOTH libraries will write out of bounds and the test will be nondeterministic and useless.
- ll/row_write_interlace: png_do_write_interlace(row_info, row, pass) for pass 0..6 over the A1 combos; it shrinks the row in place.
- ll/read_filter_row: THE HIGH-VALUE ONE. png_read_filter_row(png_ptr, row_info, row, prev_row, filter) for filter = 0..5 (and 6, 255 which are out of range and must be ignored), over every A1 combo and several widths, with randomized row and prev_row. It lazily self-initialises so a plain png_create_read_struct is enough. emit_bytes the reconstructed row each time.
- ll/check_ihdr_direct: png_check_IHDR called directly on a read struct. It warns per problem then png_errors "Invalid IHDR data", so use `pick`/`variant` - the case is registered with variants=14, so index 0..13 must each select a DIFFERENT malformed field combination (w=0, w>2^31, w>1000000, h=0, h>2^31, h>1000000, bad depths, bad color types, bad depth/color combos, bad interlace, bad compression, bad filter, and one fully VALID call as a control).
- ll/chunk_unknown_handling: png_chunk_unknown_handling for a spread of 4-byte chunk names (as u32 big-endian) after various png_set_keep_unknown_chunks settings.
- ll/user_version_check: png_user_version_check on a fresh struct with matching and mismatching version strings; print the int return and any warning.
- ll/zalloc: png_zalloc(png_ptr as *mut c_void, items, size) for sizes incl. 0 and huge, and png_zfree; print only .is_null() and free everything.
- ll/malloc_internals: png_malloc_base, png_malloc_default, png_free_default, png_malloc_array, png_realloc_array with valid arguments and with sizes that make them return NULL. Print only .is_null(). Do NOT pass nelements<=0 or element_size==0 to png_malloc_array/png_realloc_array (those png_error - out of scope here).
- ll/set_text_2: png_set_text_2 directly with valid entries, num_text 0 and negative, NULL text_ptr; print the int return and then read back with png_get_text.
- ll/warning_params: build a `png_warning_parameters` ([u8; 256]) zeroed, call png_warning_parameter / _unsigned / _signed for numbers 1..8 with various formats and values (including strings longer than 32 bytes so truncation is exercised), then png_formatted_warning with a message containing @1..@8 - the emitted warning text is the comparison.
- ll/app_error: png_app_error, png_app_warning, png_chunk_report (error levels), png_fixed_error. png_app_error/png_fixed_error are FATAL in this build, so use `pick`/`variant` (registered variants=6): 0=png_app_warning (non-fatal, loop several), 1=png_chunk_report level 0, 2=png_chunk_report level 1, 3=png_chunk_report level 2, 4=png_app_error (fatal), 5=png_fixed_error (fatal).
- ll/filter_heuristics: png_set_filter_heuristics and _fixed with various heuristic_method/num_weights/weight arrays on a write struct, then write a small image and emit_bytes it.
- ll/create_png_struct: png_create_png_struct with matching/mismatching version and with/without custom malloc/free, then png_destroy_png_struct. Print .is_null() only.
- ll/io_direct: on a write struct with our write_fn installed, call png_write_data with several buffers and png_flush; emit_bytes wbuf(). On a read struct with our read_fn installed, call png_read_data with several lengths and emit_bytes what came back. Also exercise png_default_read_data / png_default_write_data / png_default_flush via png_init_io on a real FILE* - if that is awkward, skip ONLY those three and say so in your report.
- ll/crc_direct: png_reset_crc + png_calculate_crc then png_write_chunk_start/png_write_chunk_end so the accumulated CRC is actually emitted into wbuf() and compared. Read c_src/src/pngwutil.c to get the correct call order.
- ll/write_chunks_direct: on a fresh write struct, call png_write_IHDR then each internal chunk emitter directly - png_write_PLTE, png_write_gAMA_fixed, png_write_cHRM_fixed, png_write_sRGB, png_write_sBIT, png_write_tRNS, png_write_bKGD, png_write_hIST, png_write_pHYs, png_write_oFFs, png_write_tIME, png_write_tEXt, png_write_zTXt, png_write_iTXt, png_write_sPLT, png_write_pCAL, png_write_sCAL_s, png_write_iCCP, png_write_eXIf, png_write_cICP, png_write_cLLI_fixed, png_write_mDCV_fixed, png_write_IEND - with VALID arguments, then emit_bytes wbuf(). Check each one's C definition in c_src/src/pngwutil.c for the exact argument meaning (e.g. png_write_iCCP takes the profile with its length in the first 4 bytes).
- ll/error_dispatch: png_error, png_warning, png_benign_error, png_chunk_error, png_chunk_warning, png_chunk_benign_error called directly on read and write structs. Fatal ones need variants (registered variants=8). Also exercise png_set_benign_errors(0) vs (1) so the benign escalation is compared.

